SINGLE SHARED PROMPT (HIGH impact): every model — base Mistral and all five FinGPT-mt adapters — is evaluated with one Alpaca-style make_prompt(): 'Instruction: What is the sentiment of this news? Please choose an answer from {negative/neutral/positive}
Input: {text}
Answer:'. FinGPT's official inference template (verified from AI4Finance-Foundation/FinGPT FinGPT_Benchmark) is 'Instruction: {instruction}
Input: {input}
Answer: ' but with '
Options: positive, negative' appended and the model trained on a 2-class (positive/negative) plus separate-task format — the FinGPT-mt sentiment adapters were not trained to emit the exact 3-way {negative/neutral/positive} target under this prompt without the Options line, degrading them. The Finistral adapter was itself trained with a DIFFERENT template entirely ([INST]{input}
{instruction} [/INST] {output} per Finistral_Sentiment_analyst.py line 96), yet is evaluated with the Alpaca prompt — a train/test template mismatch the paper hides (Sec 3.3 falsely claims sentences are 'wrapped exactly as in the training script').
